{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / proven performance defect\n\nThe accepted benchmark contract on closed ticket left-changeful-mamba deliberately requires 3,000 tickets, 6,000 events, exactly 24 batched commits, and at least a 10x commit-count reduction. The test is still the integration-tier long pole: 90.18s on 2026-08-01 and 93.34-107.84s on current macOS jobs, consuming 37-45% of that tier. Reducing N, event count, commit/crash safety, platform cells, or coverage is out of scope.\n\n## OSS comparison and prior decisions\n\nGit's maintained bulk-import backend consumes one stream and writes repository objects/refs in bulk rather than starting a repository write per record (git/git@745601a, Documentation/git-fast-import.adoc#L14-L29). This supports amortizing redundant repository work, but does not justify replacing rebar's event validation or crash-safe commit contract with git fast-import.\n\nTracker cross-check: left-changeful-mamba locks N=3000, 6,000 events, exactly 24 commits, >=10x reduction, rollback, and crash-resume. coercive-paediatric-bug rejects extra shards/workers that only trade wall time for runner-minutes. No prior ticket rejects removing redundant scans, serialization, or subprocess work inside the existing import batch boundary.\n\n## Plan\n\n1. Reproduce and profile before editing. Run the existing N=3000 integration node with cProfile on the current tree, plus smaller N values only to distinguish fixed, linear, and super-linear work. Record cumulative/self time and counts for importer, reducer, registry, event composition, serialization, and git subprocesses. A specific runtime hotspot and call path must be confirmed before any production edit.\n2. Scope gate. The intended change is a narrow bulk-path optimization in src/rebar/_io/import_ndjson.py. If the dominant artifact is elsewhere or would require changing durability/public semantics, stop, update file impact and this plan, and obtain a fresh plan review before editing.\n3. RED oracle. Strengthen the existing import benchmark suite with a deterministic collaborator/count or persisted-state assertion derived from the confirmed hotspot (never a wall-clock threshold). It must fail on the current redundant-work mechanism while continuing to assert 3,000 created tickets, 3,000 comments, exactly 24 commits, and >=10x reduction. Freeze and hold that oracle out from the fixer.\n4. GREEN fix. Remove only the confirmed redundant per-record work while preserving normal interactive writes and the importer's existing pass barriers, chunk size, event validation, warnings/fail-open behavior, idempotent source_id resume, rollback, and one final push. No new dependency and no test/coverage removal.\n5. Prove the oracle has teeth by restoring the redundant call path and observing RED, then restore GREEN. Run the N=3000 benchmark on the same host before/after and report time plus profiler deltas; timing is evidence, not the assertion.\n6. Regression and replication: run import batching, idempotency, crash-resume/rollback, reducer/store, lint, typecheck, and the full CI pytest selection. Confirm the benchmark contract remains byte-for-byte in force and no platform/Python matrix cell changes.\n\n## Test contract card\n\nauthoritative_contract: left-changeful-mamba and tests/interfaces/io/test_import_batch_resume.py require N=3000, 6,000 logical events, exactly 24 crash-safe commits, >=10x reduction, complete created/comment counts, rollback, and resume.\ntrigger_preconditions: a fresh temporary git-backed store importing the existing synthetic 3,000 CREATE+comment records through public rebar.import_tickets.\nproduction_path: rebar.import_tickets -> rebar._io.import_ndjson.import_tickets -> ordinary event composition -> batch_stage_and_commit.\ntest_tier: real temporary-git integration test; a unit test cannot cover commit granularity or reducer/resume semantics.\nobservable_postcondition: identical logical ticket/comment state and exactly 24 commits, plus a deterministic bound on the confirmed redundant collaborator invocation count.\nnegative_control: ordinary interactive create/comment remains one commit per event and a per-event import remains logically equivalent.\ncollateral_invariants: clean git index, no lost/duplicate events, same warnings, same source provenance.\nrecovery: injected later-chunk commit failure resumes to all tickets exactly once.\nci_gate: the integration pytest job and full Gerrit Verified gate.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The committed benchmark remains N=3000 with 6,000 events, exactly 24 commits, unchanged created/comment counts, and the >=10x reduction assertion.\n- [ ] A before/after profile on the same host identifies the dominant scaling artifact and shows lower cumulative time/call count for that artifact.\n- [ ] A deterministic non-wall-clock RED->GREEN guard pins removal of the confirmed redundant work and returns RED when the defect is re-seeded.\n- [ ] Import idempotency, crash-resume, rollback, per-event equivalence, interactive-write commit granularity, and clean-index assertions remain green.\n- [ ] No durability/public behavior, platform, Python-matrix, test, mutation, or coverage assurance is removed.\n- [ ] Focused tests, lint, typecheck, the full pytest selection, LLM-Review, and Verified pass before submit."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786638552416746001,"uuid":"2f534bd1-60e9-4ee4-b8c8-c65589687c06"}